5. WHAT THE SOFTWARE DOES ON YOUR PC

   This section describes the functions of the Software that affect your PC, so that you can
   decide whether to use them. Which personal data is processed, on which legal basis and for how
   long is set out in Part B of the Terms (the Privacy Notice), which prevails where it is more
   specific.

   a) Sign-in. You sign in on Microsoft's own page; the launcher never receives your Microsoft
      password. Sign-in tokens are sent only to Microsoft and Mojang. Signing in to the FMG
      Cosmetics server transmits the public certificate Mojang issues for your account and a
      signature made with it, which proves which Minecraft account you use. Where you use more than
      one Microsoft account, each account's sign-in is stored on your PC, encrypted with Windows
      for your Windows user account.
   b) Skins and capes. Wearing one of your Minecraft capes and changing your skin (the Skin Locker,
      in the launcher and in the game) is done with Mojang directly, as in the official launcher.
      Your skins are kept in the skins folder next to the game on your PC.
   c) Mods installed for you. At the first start of a Minecraft version, the launcher downloads
      the mods FMG Client relies on by default (currently performance mods) from Modrinth's
      servers, checks each file against the version FMG Client was tested with, and places it in
      the game's mods folder; a shader loader is downloaded when you first switch a shader on, and
      e4mc when you first host a world. Mods already in your mods folder are not removed.
   d) Weather. The weather on the Play page's background is looked up only if you enter a town
      under Settings. The launcher then sends that name to Open-Meteo once to find the place, and
      afterwards only the place's coordinates, about every 20 minutes while it is open. No location
      is ever determined automatically. Day and night are calculated on your PC.
   e) Hosting a world (Open to LAN with hosting on) runs the e4mc mod. While you host, your
      world's connection runs through e4mc's relay (e4mc.link) so that friends can join with the
      address it gives you. Nothing is hosted while your world is not open.
   f) The S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 tab.
      - Steam: you link your account on Steam's own page in your browser. The launcher never
        receives your Steam password; it receives your public Steam ID, has Steam confirm it, and
        keeps it and your public Steam name on your PC to check whether the copy of the game on
        this PC is yours. The launcher starts Steam (quietly, in the notification area) only once
        that is confirmed, namely when the launcher starts and when an update of the game is
        available; this can be switched off. Patch notes and Workshop pages are read from Steam's
        public pages; subscribing to Workshop items is carried out by Steam itself.
      - Nexus Mods: browsing needs no account. Installing needs your own Nexus Mods account: the
        personal API key you paste is stored on your PC, encrypted with Windows for your Windows
        user account, and is sent only to Nexus Mods' own interface. Downloads use only the routes
        Nexus Mods itself provides (direct for Premium members, the "Mod Manager Download" button
        for everyone else) and never a way around them. If you allow it, "Mod Manager Download"
        links open the launcher; switching this off returns them to the program that handled them
        before.
      - Pages inside the launcher: Steam's store page and Nexus Mods' pages can be displayed in a
        window inside the launcher (Microsoft Edge WebView2). They are those services' own pages:
        what you enter there, including a sign-in, goes to them only and is not read by the
        launcher. Their sign-ins are kept in the launcher's own browser folder (launcher\webview;
        deleting it signs you out). An archive you download there is kept in launcher\downloads,
        installed, and deleted after a day.
   g) Discord Rich Presence (on by default, off under Settings and under Right Shift > Discord
      Status) shows in your Discord status which game and page you are on. It communicates only
      with the Discord application running on your PC; nothing is sent while it is off or Discord
      is closed.
   h) Telemetry ("Share telemetry data") is on by default and can be switched off at any time
      under Settings > Launcher > Privacy. While it is on, the launcher sends the FMG Cosmetics
      server technical data about the installation (versions, Windows, processor, memory, graphics
      card, screens, free disk space, settings, installed mods, feature counts) and about what
      happened (starts and ends, crashes and errors, downloads, clip uploads and saved clips as
      figures - never the clip, and the notes the mod writes in the game: servers joined, frame
      rates, its own errors). Names of your Windows user and PC, profile folders and anything
      resembling a token are removed first. Chats, keystrokes, file contents, passwords, tokens,
      account details and browsing are never collected. The complete and binding description,
      including retention, legal basis and your rights, is section 21 of the Terms.
   i) Streamer Mode (Settings > Launcher) hides the town for the weather, account details and IDs
      in the launcher while you share your screen. It does not change what is transmitted.
   j) Clips. While "Record games" is on and the launcher is running (also in the notification
      area), Clips records the game that is in front of you: its picture, through Windows' own
      screen capture, and sound, through Windows' own sound capture. Nothing is read from or
      inserted into the game, and recording stops shortly after the game leaves the foreground. By
      default all sound your PC plays is recorded (this can include voice chat and music); you can
      limit it to the game's own sound, and you can add your microphone, which is off by default
      (Windows shows when the microphone is in use). Only the last seconds, the clip length you
      chose, are kept in a buffer on your PC that is overwritten continuously and deleted when
      recording stops; the save key (F7 unless you change it) saves them as an MP4 in your clips
      folder. Clips stay on your PC unless you share one by link; nothing is uploaded
      automatically. Games can be excluded one by one under Clips > Settings. The "Allow it"
      button there, only when you press it, sets Windows' microphone permission for desktop
      programs to "allowed" for your own Windows user account and nothing else; Windows' Settings
      (Privacy > Microphone) can reverse it at any time. Your legal responsibility for what you
      record and share is set out in section 8 of the Terms.
   k) Starting with Windows (on by default; Settings > Launcher > Windows). The launcher adds
      itself to the start-up programs of your own Windows user account (one value under
      HKEY_CURRENT_USER\...\Run, no administrator rights) and starts hidden in the notification
      area so that Clips is ready; switching the option off removes that value. "Keep running in
      the tray" keeps the launcher there when you close its window; its icon's menu ends it.
   l) Import from other clients, only when you start it, reads - and never changes - the settings,
      servers and packs of other Minecraft launchers and clients on your PC and copies them into
      FMG Client. Their account files are never opened.
   m) Emote sounds. Emotes may play a sound on your PC and on the PCs of FMG Client players near
      you. A sound file you place in the emote-sounds folder of the game folder replaces an
      emote's sound on your own PC only; it is never transmitted. You are responsible for having
      the right to use files you place there.
   n) Updates. The launcher checks for new versions when it starts and installs them
      automatically. The Software is never replaced while the game is running.
   o) Live Ping (in the Ping mod, on by default) measures your ping every few seconds the way
      Minecraft's own server list does: a short, separate status request to the server you play
      on. The server can see these requests like a server-list refresh; nothing is sent over the
      connection you play on. It can be switched off in the Ping mod's settings.


